    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Hi, last night I started investigating this behavior and working on a fix.\n\nHowever, while reviewing the documentation this morning to update it with the fixes, a question came up. This parameter controls how the `statusline` of the **\"last window\"** behaves—specifically the bottom-most window, the one closest to the command line. Given this, the current behavior makes sense: if `laststatus=3`, all windows share a single global statusline (associated with the \"last window\"), showing only a divider between splits; if `laststatus=0`, there is no statusline on the \"last window,\" meaning the statusline appears on all windows *except* those touching the command line.\n\nI haven't found a way to completely hide the statuslines for all splits; you can try something like `set laststatus=0 | set noshowmode | set noruler | set noshowcmd`, but that doesn't completely remove the status line, It only hide some information, so the line for the status still retains its space.\n\nCould someone with more experience please confirm:\n1. Is my interpretation of `laststatus` correct?\n2. Is there any way to completely hide the statusline?\n3. If the answer to the previous point is no: are there any implications to implementing such a feature—for instance, the user voluntarily giving up access to buffer information?\n\nTks. :)",
